This example runs saltfm by hand instead of through the TAU compiler scripts.
saltfm writes an instrumented copy of the source, and the Makefile compiles
it with the flags from the TAU Makefile:

$ saltfm matmult.c -o matmult.inst.c --
$ clang -g -O2 $(TAU_INCLUDE) $(TAU_DEFS) -c matmult.inst.c -o matmult.o
$ clang++ -g matmult.o -o matmult $(TAU_LIBS)

Give compiler flags, such as -I and -D options, to saltfm after "--". The
program is linked with the C++ compiler because the TAU library needs the
C++ runtime. To use it, with saltfm on your PATH (spack load saltfm):
$ export TAU_MAKEFILE=<taudir>/x86_64/lib/Makefile.tau-clang
$ ./run.sh
